## Proof

Let \(G\) be a finitely presented group, with a finite presentation
\[
G \;=\; \langle\, g_{1},\dots,g_{n}\;\mid\; r_{1},\dots,r_{m}\,\rangle,
\]
where each \(r_{j}\) is a word in the generators \(g_{1},\dots,g_{n}\) and their inverses. We construct a 2-dimensional CW-complex \(K\) — the **presentation complex** of the presentation — and show that \(\pi_{1}(K)\cong G\).

### Construction of \(K\)

**Step 1 (0-skeleton).** Take a single 0-cell (a single vertex):
\[
K^{(0)} \;=\; \{v\}.
\]

**Step 2 (1-skeleton).** For each generator \(g_{i}\) (\(1\le i\le n\)), attach a 1-cell \(e_{i}^{1}\) to \(v\): both endpoints of \(e_{i}^{1}\) are glued to \(v\), so each \(e_{i}^{1}\) becomes a loop at \(v\). The 1-skeleton is
\[
K^{(1)} \;=\; \bigvee_{i=1}^{n} S^{1}_{i},
\]
a wedge (one-point union) of \(n\) circles, one circle for each generator. This is a graph with a single vertex \(v\) and \(n\) edges, hence a connected 1-dimensional CW-complex.

**Step 3 (2-skeleton).** For each relator \(r_{j}\) (\(1\le j\le m\)), write \(r_{j}\) as a reduced word
\[
r_{j} \;=\; g_{i_{1}}^{\varepsilon_{1}}\,g_{i_{2}}^{\varepsilon_{2}}\,\cdots\,g_{i_{\ell_{j}}}^{\varepsilon_{\ell_{j}}}, \qquad \varepsilon_{k}\in\{+1,-1\}.
\]
This word specifies a closed loop in the 1-skeleton \(K^{(1)}\): starting at \(v\), traverse the edge \(e_{i_{k}}^{1}\) in the forward direction if \(\varepsilon_{k}=+1\) and in the reverse direction if \(\varepsilon_{k}=-1\), for \(k=1,\dots,\ell_{j}\). This yields a continuous map
\[
\varphi_{j}\colon S^{1}\;\longrightarrow\;K^{(1)},
\]
i.e. an attaching map for a 2-cell. Attach a 2-cell \(e_{j}^{2}\) (a copy of the open 2-disk \(D^{2}\)) to \(K^{(1)}\) along \(\varphi_{j}\):
\[
K \;=\; K^{(2)} \;=\; K^{(1)}\;\cup_{\varphi_{1}}\,e_{1}^{2}\;\cup_{\varphi_{2}}\,e_{2}^{2}\;\cdots\;\cup_{\varphi_{m}}\,e_{m}^{2}.
\]

The result \(K\) is a finite 2-dimensional CW-complex (hence a 2-complex) with:
- one 0-cell,
- \(n\) 1-cells (one per generator),
- \(m\) 2-cells (one per relator).

### Verification that \(\pi_{1}(K)\cong G\)

**The 1-skeleton.** The 1-skeleton \(K^{(1)}=\bigvee_{i=1}^{n}S^{1}_{i}\) is a connected graph with a single vertex. The fundamental group of a connected graph is a free group, with one free generator per edge not in a maximal tree. Since there is a single vertex, the maximal tree is just \(\{v\}\) (no edges), so every edge contributes a free generator. Choosing the loop \(e_{i}^{1}\) as the generator corresponding to \(g_{i}\):
\[
\pi_{1}(K^{(1)},\,v)\;\cong\;F(g_{1},\dots,g_{n}),
\]
the free group of rank \(n\), with the isomorphism sending the homotopy class of the loop \(e_{i}^{1}\) to the free generator \(g_{i}\).

**Effect of attaching a 2-cell.** We use the standard fact about the effect on \(\pi_{1}\) of attaching a 2-cell. Let \(X\) be a path-connected space with basepoint \(x_{0}\), and let \(f\colon S^{1}\to X\) be an attaching map representing the element \([f]\in\pi_{1}(X,x_{0})\). Form \(X' = X\cup_{f}D^{2}\). Then the inclusion \(X\hookrightarrow X'\) induces a surjection
\[
\pi_{1}(X,x_{0})\;\twoheadrightarrow\;\pi_{1}(X',x_{0}),
\]
whose kernel is the **normal closure** of \([f]\) in \(\pi_{1}(X,x_{0})\):
\[
\pi_{1}(X',x_{0})\;\cong\;\pi_{1}(X,x_{0})\,\big/\,\langle\!\langle\,[f]\,\rangle\!\rangle.
\]

*(Justification:* The 2-disk is simply connected and its boundary is attached along \([f]\), so by the Seifert–van Kampen theorem — writing \(X'=X\cup D^{2}\) with intersection a collar neighborhood of the boundary circle, which deformation-retracts onto the loop \([f]\) — the new fundamental group is the quotient of \(\pi_{1}(X)\) by the normal subgroup generated by the class of the attaching loop. Amalgamation with the trivial group \(\pi_{1}(D^{2})=1\) forces the attaching loop to become trivial, and taking the normal closure accounts for all conjugates that must also vanish.)*

**Attaching all the 2-cells.** Apply this iteratively. Start with \(X_{0}=K^{(1)}\), \(\pi_{1}(X_{0})=F(g_{1},\dots,g_{n})\). The attaching map \(\varphi_{1}\) of the first 2-cell spells out the word \(r_{1}\) in the loops \(e_{i}^{1}\), so under the isomorphism \(\pi_{1}(K^{(1)})\cong F(g_{1},\dots,g_{n})\), the class \([\varphi_{1}]\) corresponds to the element \(r_{1}\in F(g_{1},\dots,g_{n})\). Attaching \(e_{1}^{2}\) gives
\[
\pi_{1}(X_{1})\;\cong\;F(g_{1},\dots,g_{n})\,\big/\,\langle\!\langle\,r_{1}\,\rangle\!\rangle.
\]
Attaching \(e_{2}^{2}\) along \(\varphi_{2}\) (spelling \(r_{2}\)) quotients further by the normal closure of the image of \(r_{2}\):
\[
\pi_{1}(X_{2})\;\cong\;F(g_{1},\dots,g_{n})\,\big/\,\langle\!\langle\,r_{1},\,r_{2}\,\rangle\!\rangle,
\]
and so on. After attaching all \(m\) two-cells,
\[
\pi_{1}(K,\,v)\;=\;\pi_{1}(X_{m})\;\cong\;F(g_{1},\dots,g_{n})\,\big/\,\langle\!\langle\,r_{1},\,r_{2},\,\dots,\,r_{m}\,\rangle\!\rangle.
\]

**Conclusion.** The right-hand side is, by definition of a group presentation, precisely the group \(G\):
\[
\pi_{1}(K,\,v)\;\cong\;\langle\,g_{1},\dots,g_{n}\;\mid\;r_{1},\dots,r_{m}\,\rangle\;=\;G.
\]

Thus the presentation complex \(K\) — built from one vertex, one loop per generator, and one 2-cell per relator attached along the loop spelling out that relator — is a 2-complex with \(\pi_{1}(K)\cong G\).

### Summary of the construction

Given \(G=\langle g_{1},\dots,g_{n}\mid r_{1},\dots,r_{m}\rangle\):

1. **One vertex** \(v\).
2. **One loop (1-cell) \(e_{i}^{1}\) at \(v\) for each generator \(g_{i}\)**, giving the 1-skeleton \(\bigvee_{i=1}^{n}S^{1}\), whose fundamental group is the free group \(F(g_{1},\dots,g_{n})\).
3. **One 2-cell \(e_{j}^{2}\) for each relator \(r_{j}\)**, attached along the closed edge-path in the 1-skeleton obtained by reading the word \(r_{j}\) in the loops \(e_{i}^{1}\) (using \(e_{i}^{1}\) for \(g_{i}\) and its reverse for \(g_{i}^{-1}\)).

The 1-skeleton contributes the free group on the generators, and each 2-cell kills the normal closure of the corresponding relator, so the resulting 2-complex has fundamental group exactly \(G\). ∎

